think Crux-first pick among the card's named candidates: the two-click READING-ORDER relation is the sharpest genuinely-new move -- the click family reads groups, reading_order reads a total object sequence, and the two had never been composed. A marquee-facts read would largely duplicate selection_cluster_facts; a two-click nearest was already covered in click_relation's distance.
think Designed image/click_order.rye: each click resolves to its group (hit_test + overlap_clusters' cluster_of), and a group reads at the rank of its FIRST-appearing member (min reading_rank). click_group_min_rank, click_group_lead_label, click_reads_before, over a scene whose object order is [P1, Q, P2] with P1,P2 one group and Q reading between them.
obs Red-then-green in the same round: the first metal run failed SceneNotThreeComponents -- Q's block at (9,3) was 8-adjacent diagonally to P2's arm at (8,4), merging them. Shifted Q two columns right to box (10,2)-(11,3), 8-disconnected from P2 while its box still overlaps neither. GREEN on the next run, no code red -- a scene-build red the label count caught, exactly the machine-catches-it pattern REDS names.
obs The crux proves two sharp facts: the group order is INVARIANT to the tap site (tapping P1 rank 1 or P2 rank 3 both read group min rank 1, lead label 1), and it DISAGREES with the tapped-object order (a tap on P2 then Q reads click_reads_before TRUE -- G1 before G2 -- yet reads_before(P2,Q) is FALSE, Q before P2 as objects; only each group's minimum decides). A strict total order over distinct groups; SameGroup/EmptyClick/PointOutOfBounds refusals by name; determinism.
